<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ixtlilxochitl I</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/1418">1418</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ixtlilxochitl I was ruler of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/acolhua">Acolhua</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/texcoco">Texcoco</a> in the early 15th century. He resisted the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tepanecs">Tepanec</a> ruler <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tezozomoc">Tezozomoc</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/azcapotzalco">Azcapotzalco</a> and claimed the title of chichimecatecuhtli, but the Tepanecs overran Texcoco and killed him in 1418. His son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nezahualcoyotl">Nezahualcoyotl</a> escaped and later recovered the kingdom with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexica">Mexica</a> help.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ixtlilxochitl-i.jpg" alt="Ixtlilxochitl I" width="209" height="396" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Bibliothèque Nationale, Paris · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ixtlilxochitl_I.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Ixtlilxochitl Ome Tochtli, born about 1380, claimed descent from Xolotl and Nopaltzin, Chichimec chieftains of legend, and became tlatoani of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/texcoco">Texcoco</a> in 1409 on the death of his father, Techotlala. For several years he went on paying tribute to the powerful <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tepanecs">Tepanec</a> city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/azcapotzalco">Azcapotzalco</a> and its ruler <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tezozomoc">Tezozomoc</a>. Growing restive, he passed over a daughter of Tezozomoc and instead married the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexica">Mexica</a> princess Matlalcihuatzin, a daughter of Huitzilihuitl, tlatoani of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tenochtitlan">Tenochtitlan</a>.</p><p>In 1414 he took the title Chichimeca Tecuhtli, Lord of the Chichimecs, and urged the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexica">Mexica</a> to join him against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/azcapotzalco">Azcapotzalco</a>. Huitzilihuitl, whose own wife was a daughter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tezozomoc">Tezozomoc</a>, perhaps out of deference to her kept his support for Azcapotzalco. Tezozomoc then led a large army, with Mexica forces among it, against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/texcoco">Texcoco</a>, but after some early successes he was driven back. Ixtlilxochitl carried the war to Azcapotzalco and besieged it for several months, but could not take the city and returned home.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>The following year <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tepanecs">Tepanec</a> forces, again with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexica">Mexica</a> contingents, besieged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/texcoco">Texcoco</a> and drove Ixtlilxochitl out. He was cornered in the foothills of Mount Tlaloc and killed there, and his young son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nezahualcoyotl">Nezahualcoyotl</a> watched his death from the branches of a nearby tree. Texcoco was given to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tenochtitlan">Tenochtitlan</a> as a tributary, and Nezahualcoyotl fled into exile in Huexotzingo. Ten years later he avenged his father and retook Texcoco with the help of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/itzcoatl">Itzcoatl</a>, the future tlatoani of Tenochtitlan, and became known as the poet-king.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ixtlilxochitl I” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1418 the Tepanec Empire killed its ruler <b>Ixtlilxochitl I</b> and occupied it, forcing his son Nezahualcoyotl into exile.</blockquote><a href="/state/texcoco">Texcoco</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Texcoco de Mora, in what is now <a href="/countries/mexico">Mexico</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=19.51,-98.88&amp;year=1408" aria-label="Texcoco de Mora on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Ixtlilxochitl I"><image href="/maps/globes/g270n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4287 5864h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4287 5864h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=19.51,-98.88&amp;year=1408">What was here in 1408? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
